course: dictionary
lesson: 12849
title: Endorse: saying you support something
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To endorse someone or something means to say, out loud or in public, that you support or approve of them. «The head teacher endorsed our plan for a school garden.» The clue is that she agreed with the plan in front of other people, so endorsed means approved.
explain: Adverts often use this word. «The famous footballer endorses this brand of trainers» means he is paid to say the trainers are good. The clues are famous, paid and trainers, so you can guess that endorse means to recommend.
explain: Endorse has a special meaning in money and law. To endorse a cheque means to sign your name on the back of it. The clue is that it is a document you sign, not a person giving an opinion.
fact: Endorse means to say publicly that you support or approve of someone or something.
fact: «Endorse» is often used with «a product», «a candidate» or «a plan».
fact: «Fully endorse» means to support something completely.
fact: A celebrity endorsement is often paid, so it is not proof that a product is good.
fact: To endorse a cheque means to sign it on the back.
exercise: «The mayor said she fully endorsed the new bike lanes.» What does endorsed mean? | Painted in bright colours | *Publicly supported or approved | Asked a question about | Took away
exercise: «The famous runner is paid to endorse a brand of trainers.» Why is the runner paid? | To sell her own house | *To recommend the trainers in an advert | To run in a race for the school | To give the trainers away for free
exercise: What does it mean to endorse a cheque? | *Sign your name on the back | Tear it in half | Write a number on the front | Put it in a drawer
test: Nadia says endorse means to hate something. Is she right? | Yes, it means to dislike very strongly | Yes, it means to forget something | *No, it means to publicly support or approve | No, it means to buy something
test: Which word is closest in meaning to endorse? | forget | *approve | hide | delay
exam: Mia's teacher says, «I endorse your idea for the class quiz.» What does the teacher mean? | *She publicly approves of Mia's idea | She wants Mia to sign a cheque | She has lost the idea | She is cross with Mia
